Q2. Apache Deployment

Create a deployment using image httpd, name apache-deployment. Verify using kubectl get deployments and kubectl get pods.

bash
kubectl create deployment apache-deployment --image=httpd
kubectl get deployments ; kubectl get pods

Q3. Increase and Decrease Replicas

Increase replicas to 4. Verify ReplicaSet and Pods. Decrease replicas to 2. Verify with kubectl get rs and kubectl get pods.

bash
kubectl scale deployment apache-deployment --replicas=4
kubectl get rs ; kubectl get pods        # rs = ReplicaSet
kubectl scale deployment apache-deployment --replicas=2
kubectl get rs ; kubectl get pods

Q4. Use YAML File

Create Apache Pod, Deployment, and Replica configuration using YAML. Write a YAML file for Apache Deployment, image httpd, replicas 3. Apply with kubectl apply -f apache-deployment.yaml. Verify all resources.

apache-deployment.yaml:

yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: apache-deployment      # if it exists from Q2: kubectl delete deployment apache-deployment first
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels: {app: apache}
  template:
    metadata:
      labels: {app: apache}    # must match the selector above
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: apache
        image: httpd
        ports: [{containerPort: 80}]
bash
kubectl apply -f apache-deployment.yaml
kubectl get all                          # deployment, replicaset and pods

Q5. ClusterIP Service

Expose Apache Deployment using ClusterIP. Service name apache-clusterip, port 80. Test service access inside the cluster.

bash
kubectl expose deployment apache-deployment --name=apache-clusterip --type=ClusterIP --port=80
kubectl get svc
kubectl run test --image=busybox --rm -it --restart=Never -- wget -qO- apache-clusterip
# a temporary pod calls the service by name; the output is "It works!"

