Q8. Flask Application Deployment

Create a simple Python Flask application. (1) Flask app to display PRN number and student name. (2) Create Dockerfile. (3) Build Docker image. (4) Deploy Flask app as a Kubernetes Deployment. (5) Create a Service to expose it. (6) Access output in browser.

app.py:

python
from flask import Flask
app = Flask(__name__)
@app.route("/")
def home():
    return "PRN: <PRN><br>Name: <Your Name>"
app.run(host="0.0.0.0", port=5000)   # 0.0.0.0 so it is reachable from outside the container

Dockerfile:

dockerfile
FROM python:3.12-slim
WORKDIR /app
COPY app.py .
RUN pip install flask
CMD ["python", "app.py"]
bash
eval $(minikube docker-env)          # point your Docker CLI at minikube's Docker so it sees the image
docker build -t flask-app:v1 .

flask-deploy.yaml:

yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata: {name: flask-deployment}
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector: {matchLabels: {app: flask}}
  template:
    metadata: {labels: {app: flask}}
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: flask
        image: flask-app:v1
        imagePullPolicy: Never       # use the local image, don't try to pull it
        ports: [{containerPort: 5000}]
bash
kubectl apply -f flask-deploy.yaml
kubectl expose deployment flask-deployment --type=NodePort --port=5000
minikube service flask-deployment    # opens the browser

Q9. Rolling Update Flask Application

Modify Flask app output to include PRN, student name and course name. Build new Docker image version. Update the Kubernetes Deployment with the new image. Verify using kubectl rollout status deployment/flask-deployment. Access updated output in browser.

Change the return line to add <br>Course: <Course Name>, then:

bash
docker build -t flask-app:v2 .       # still inside minikube's docker-env
kubectl set image deployment/flask-deployment flask=flask-app:v2   # container-name=new-image
kubectl rollout status deployment/flask-deployment   # shows pods being replaced gradually

Refresh the browser to see the new output.

⚡ Cheat tips

Dry-run YAML generator: kubectl create deployment x --image=httpd --replicas=3 --dry-run=client -o yaml > x.yaml. It prints a ready-made manifest, so you never type YAML from scratch.
alias k=kubectl saves typing.
ErrImagePull or ImagePullBackOff on the Flask app means you forgot eval $(minikube docker-env) before building, or forgot imagePullPolicy: Never.
kubectl rollout undo deployment/flask-deployment rolls back.
kubectl delete -f file.yaml removes everything a file created.
The selector.matchLabels must match template.labels, or apply fails.

